## Why This Is Asked
A practical beginner-level check for implementing and validating security controls in Kubernetes using policy engines, focusing on least privilege and safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Pod Security Standards Baseline/Restricted
- Policy engines: Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper
- Privilege escalation controls: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities
- Testing methods: compliant vs non-compliant pods, namespace scoping
- Change management and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Conceptual Kyverno policy (JS object for illustration)
const policy = {
  apiVersion: 'kyverno/v1',
  kind: 'ClusterPolicy',
  metadata: { name: 'require-baseline-security' },
  spec: {
    rules: [
      {
        name: 'require-baseline',
        match: { resources: { kinds: ['Pod'] }},
        validate: {
          message: 'Pods must set runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem and avoid hostPath',
          pattern: {
            spec: {
              containers: { any: { securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true } }},
              hostPath: { absent: {} }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exceptions for legitimate hostPath usage?
- How would you monitor for policy violations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluate hands-on ability to translate policy into Gatekeeper and Kyverno, using SPIFFE for workload identity and enforcing data-scoping in a real multi-tenant setup.

## Key Concepts
- Gatekeeper ConstraintTemplate and Constraint
- Kyverno policy enforcement
- SPIFFE IDs for notebook workloads
- Data catalog whitelisting and kernel/runtime controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-check illustrating the policy guard
function isCompliant(pod) {
  const spiffe = pod?.metadata?.annotations?.['spiffe.io/id'];
  const mounts = (pod?.spec?.containers?.[0]?.volumeMounts || [])
    .map(m => m.mountPath);
  const hasWhitelistedData = mounts.some(p => p.startsWith('/data/whitelist'));
  const hasApprovedKernel = !!spiffe && spiffe.startsWith('spiffe://')
    && (pod?.spec?.containers?.[0]?.image || '').includes('approved-runtime');
  return hasWhitelistedData && hasApprovedKernel;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate policy effectiveness in CI/CD and in prod?
- How would you handle exceptions for legitimate data-science projects without widening risk?
